  <question type="multichoice">
    <name><text>Q1: If statement—basic behaviour</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[In Java, what happens with the statements inside the braces of an <code>if</code> statement?<br><br><pre><code>if (condition) {
    // statements
}</code></pre>]]></text></questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[]]></text></generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>1.000000</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.3333333</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <single>true</single>
    <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
    <answernumbering>abc</answernumbering>

    <answer fraction="100">
      <text>They execute only if the boolean condition evaluates to <code>true</code>.</text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[Correct — the <code>if</code> block runs only when the condition is true.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0">
      <text>They always execute once, regardless of the condition.</text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[Not quite — the block is skipped if the condition is false.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0">
      <text>They execute repeatedly until the condition becomes false.</text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[No — an <code>if</code> is not a loop; it runs at most once.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0">
      <text>They execute only after the <code>else</code> block completes.</text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[Incorrect — the <code>else</code> block is mutually exclusive with the <code>if</code> block.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
  </question>
